From 24a2a8f1a1c1d9e0a1572a9946190c135c804b18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 03:41:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf: decompress each zstd payload at once when unpacking

Unpacking spent most of its CPU time in zstd's streaming decoder rather than
in decoding: `lgz::decompress` reads a byte at a time, and each read from a
`zstd::stream::Decoder` is a call into `ZSTD_decompressStream`. Now each
payload is decompressed into memory with one call, then decoded from the
slice.

Also, when unpacking:
* each thread keeps its zstd contexts across files, with the dictionary
  loaded once, rather than parsing the 475 KiB dictionary for every archive;
* `leantar -x` reads each archive with one read and decodes its payloads in
  place, rather than copying them out of a `BufReader`;
* a zstd-compressed file (`.ilean`, `.ir`, a trace) is written with one
  write, rather than in 8 KiB pieces;
* unpacking a single archive (as Lake does, one process per module) does
  not start a thread per CPU;
* unpacked files get the permissions of a new file (0666 less the umask)
  again, rather than the 0600 of the temporary files they are renamed from.

Unpacking the 27,538 archives of a real build (16 GB of outputs) on 4 CPUs
takes 22 CPU-seconds of user time rather than 63, and its outputs are byte
for byte the same. The archive format and packing are unchanged.

